First, I should think about the overall structure of the script. It needs to:
1. Import three.js.
2. Define utility functions (clamp, lerp, easing, RNG, canvas texture generator, keyframe track, bump function) – these will be reused throughout.
3. Set up the renderer, scene, camera, and handle resize.
4. Create realistic lighting: a directional sun light (golden hour), a hemisphere sky light, a bounce light, and a point lamp near the nightstand.
5. Build the set procedurally: floor with wood planks, walls with paint texture, window with frame, glass, curtains, light shaft, baseboards, ceiling.
6. Add props: rug, bed, nightstand with lamp, toy chest, bookshelf, posters, scattered toys (ball, teddy), building blocks that will be knocked over.
7. Create the two main characters: a tin wind‑up mouse (Pip) with body, snout, eyes, whiskers, key, wheels, tail; and a plush cat (Biscuit) with body, head, ears, eyes, tail, legs.
8. Define the choreography: paths for mouse and cat as CatmullRom curves, with keyframe tracks controlling progress, speed, spring energy, etc. Include functions for heading, angle interpolation, tail placement, and per‑frame actor updates (position, rotation, key winding, wheel rotation, tail animation, cat hopping, ear movement, etc.).
9. Implement block physics: each block has a rest position and a scripted tumble triggered at the moment the mouse crashes through them.
10. Set up the cinematography: a list of shots with start/end times, FOV, optional shake, and an update function that positions the camera based on a normalized progress value n.
11. Add sound: a simple audio context that plays a low‑level room tone and generates wind‑up ticks based on the mouse’s spring energy, plus crash sounds.
12. Overlays: title, subtitles, fade, timecode, grain effect.
13. Playback loop: requestAnimationFrame, dt calculation, update all subsystems, render, and UI for start/stop.

